{"id": "tea:siddhitraya:atmasiddhi", "source": "src:siddhitraya", "location": {"ref": "Ātmasiddhi"}, "paraphrase": "The self is distinct from the body, the senses, the mind, the vital breath and cognition; it is established in the awareness 'I', is self-luminous, eternal, atomic and many, and is the knower of its cognitions.", "tags": {"level": "ultimate", "standpoint": "analytic", "naya": null, "path": ["knowledge"], "stage": "all"}, "types": ["consciousness-mind"], "concepts": ["cpt:aham-artha", "cpt:jiva"], "disputes": ["dsp:is-there-a-self"], "teachers": ["tch:yamuna"], "speaker": "Yāmuna", "reported_by_opponent": false, "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U14-visistadvaita"], "disagreements": []}, "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U14-visistadvaita"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:siddhitraya:isvarasiddhi", "source": "src:siddhitraya", "location": {"ref": "Īśvarasiddhi"}, "paraphrase": "Yāmuna argues for the existence of a Lord who is the maker of the world; whether he accepts the Naiyāyika inference or rests the proof on scripture alone (as Rāmānuja does) is not settled from memory here.", "tags": {"level": "ultimate", "standpoint": "analytic", "naya": null, "path": ["knowledge"], "stage": "unmarked"}, "types": ["ultimate", "dispute"], "disputes": ["dsp:isvara"], "notes": "Low confidence on Yāmuna's exact stance; check the text.", "teachers": ["tch:yamuna"], "speaker": "Yāmuna", "reported_by_opponent": false, "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U14-visistadvaita"], "disagreements": []}, "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "low", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U14-visistadvaita"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:siddhitraya:samvitsiddhi", "source": "src:siddhitraya", "location": {"ref": "Saṃvitsiddhi"}, "paraphrase": "Against the Advaitins, consciousness without subject or object is never found; consciousness is always someone's and of something, and the non-dual texts do not deny the plurality of real things.", "tags": {"level": "ultimate", "standpoint": "polemical", "naya": null, "path": ["knowledge"], "stage": "unmarked"}, "types": ["consciousness-mind", "dispute"], "disputes": ["dsp:world-real-or-appearance"], "notes": "The Saṃvitsiddhi survives in part; content recalled from citations.", "teachers": ["tch:yamuna"], "speaker": "Yāmuna", "reported_by_opponent": false, "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U14-visistadvaita"], "disagreements": []}, "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "low", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U14-visistadvaita"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
